From 3a6507bfbfa5a296d3c0cab7c03c56f947f98131 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: reaper <theunbounddeveloper@outlook.com>
Date: Sun, 30 Aug 2026 10:28:56 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] field fixes: AiMesh search, restore lockout, and every Apply
 acknowledges itself

Nine owner-reported or field-proven defects, all small, all in the first-run /
recovery area. Nothing here changes enforcement semantics except the Gatekeeper
restore deferral, which is the point of it.

AIMESH "ADD NODE" FINDS NOTHING (P1, field 2026-08-30, GT-BE98 CAP + 3x RT-AX92U)
select_best_onboarding_re() required ALL FIVE of rssi/rssi_5g/rssi_5g2/source/
ts_eth before a candidate parent contributed anything - and cfg_server only
writes the bands it actually HEARD the node on. A CAP that heard a node on
2.4 GHz alone discarded every parent, so no parent was selected and the node
never reached the list. The 2.4 GHz fallback in the obPriority block was already
correct and simply unreachable. The gate existed to avoid dereferencing missing
keys, not because all bands are required: sourceObj stays mandatory, ts_eth is
required only on the ethernet branch that reads it, and each band is guarded
individually. These gates affect the SEARCH LISTING only - the join path never
reads onboarding.json. Explains the long-standing "works for some, not others"
shape: it depended on whether the CAP happened to hear the node on 5 GHz.
The v2.3.7 aimesh: diagnostic logging is the only reason this was diagnosable.

A SETTINGS RESTORE CAN NO LONGER LOCK ANYONE OUT (P1, field 2026-08-29)
A full-backup restore is two phases: the nvram half arrives with the reboot,
the /jffs half is replayed from restore_pending.json afterwards. gk_enable came
back in phase 1, the device list not until phase 2 - so the box rebooted with
Gatekeeper armed and an EMPTY list: default-deny for every device on the LAN,
including the operator, who had SSH off and got back in only through the HTTPS
admin-port carve-out. Two layers:
- start_gk() now holds enforcement OFF while restore_pending.json exists, with
  a syslog line naming the fix (finish the restore on the backup page). Not
  silent: the dashboard Gatekeeper tile keys off gk_enable, and the backup page
  banners the pending restore. All three re-arm paths are structurally closed
  while deferred - gk_refresh() and the firewall hook gate on apply.sh existing
  (stop_gk unlinked it; the deferral never regenerates it) and gkd escalates
  via restart_gk, which lands back in the deferral. Phase 2 issues restart_gk
  after the restored list lands; the pending file is consumed valid or corrupt,
  so nothing can wedge the deferral on.
- The operator's MAC (connection IP -> ARP, via the new reaper_client_mac())
  is stamped approved-full in phase 1 and re-stamped after the phase-2 import
  replaces the list - covering the case where the archive predates the
  operator's device, or their MAC changed (a Wi-Fi password change makes client
  OSes present a fresh randomized MAC).

EVERY RESTORE RESULT IS NOW VISIBLE
The backup card's msg() helper began `if(!m)return;` - with #rbk_msg absent the
outcome of a restore was discarded with no trace, success or failure. That is
why a field restore could POST, receive 200 OK and appear to do nothing, and
why the v2.9.2 per-cause ec codes never reached the operator. Falls back to
alert() + console so a result can never be swallowed again.

EVERY APPLY ACKNOWLEDGES ITSELF
- Advanced_Wireless_Content.asp raised its loading overlay INSIDE the nvramSet
  success callback - applying a wireless security change deauths the client, so
  the response never arrives, the callback never runs, and the page sits inert
  while the router applies the change perfectly ("it appeared nothing happened
  when in reality it rebooted the wifi with the password"). This is the
  FIRST-BOOT path now that factory Wi-Fi is open. Overlay and reload are armed
  BEFORE the request; a security change adds the stock DrSurf_sweet_advise1
  disconnect notice (already translated in all 25 packs) to the overlay.
- Reaper_Firewall.asp fwApply() posted into a hidden iframe then blindly
  reloaded 7 s later - seven call sites (URL filter, keyword filter, log level,
  four rule editors) all looked like dead buttons. A toast (stock
  Main_alert_proceeding_desc1) is raised before the submit. The Rules-tab Apply
  is a different path that already reports via showSaved() and is untouched.
- The Status tab's Refresh gave no sign it did anything - identical innerHTML
  rewritten over identical cards. The click is acknowledged at once and the
  shared #st_hint span is stamped with the time on xhr()'s single always-fires
  completion point (standing rule 34: no busy flag cleared only on success -
  loadStatus is shared with the 10 s auto-refresh and a failed poll would wedge
  the button).

ALSO
- get_fh_ap_ssid_by_unit() had no else under RTCONFIG_MULTILAN_MWL: when no
  fronthaul prefix resolves (factory box before cfg_server creates the MAINFH
  profiles; 6 GHz with no .1 VIF) it returned an EMPTY string instead of
  falling back to wlN_ssid the way the non-MWL path always does. Consumers are
  the AiMesh fronthaul SSID reporters, so it misreported during onboarding.
- reaper_diag: the preamble "0. SANITIZATION LEDGER" and "0b. FINDINGS" lose
  their numbers (owner decision 2026-08-29: drop, do NOT renumber the body -
  every by-number reference in prose, comments, memory and session rows stays
  valid). Reaper_Diag.asp's sample line matches again. REAPER-DIAG v1.3.9.
- Reaper_GK.asp: the guest-pass length select showed a bare 4/8/24/48 with
  nothing saying what they were, while the per-device grant button rendered a
  hardcoded English " h". Both now go through new token RGK_61 (English-seeded
  "h" in all 25 packs, appended so no line index moves; translate pass owed).

Rides along, already in tree: REAPER-DIAG v1.3.8 counter fixes (QoS shaper
readback + 19b event dedup), Warden custom-feed ceilings, RFW_* IPv6 labels.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
